    description: "The Bed 4 apple is both the structural centre of the planting and a working fruit tree. Pink buds open to white blossom in April, supporting early pollinators before leaves fill the crown; fruit then develops through summer for a late-summer or autumn harvest. Its cultivar and rootstock are not recorded, so final size, pollination group, flavour and exact picking date must come from observing this tree rather than generic apple assumptions.",
    months: ["Apr", "May"], size: "Rootstock dependent", sizeDetail: "Measure crown spread yearly until cultivar and rootstock are known", position: "Full sun", positionDetail: "Open light supports blossom, ripening and airflow", soil: "Fertile, moisture-retentive", soilDetail: "Well-drained loam; mulch without touching the trunk", hardiness: "Fully hardy", hardinessDetail: "Blossom can still be damaged by late frost", featureLabel: "Harvest", feature: "Late summer–autumn", featureDetail: "Picking window and use still being recorded", identity: "Malus domestica", identityDetail: "Cultivar and rootstock unknown",
    under: "small fruit, premature fruit drop and curled leaves in summer", over: "yellow growth or dieback while the soil remains saturated", spring: "Inspect blossom after frost, mulch the moist root zone and avoid insecticide use while pollinators are working.", summer: "Thin congested fruitlets after the June drop, water through drought and carry out only suitable trained-tree pruning.", autumn: "Taste-test and record the picking window; remove damaged windfalls and store only sound fruit.", winter: "Prune a free-standing tree for an open crown, removing dead, diseased and crossing wood without over-thinning.",
    about: "Domestic apples are grafted trees: the named fruiting cultivar determines the apples, while the rootstock largely controls vigour and mature size. Most crop better with compatible blossom nearby. Until a fruit label or confident identification is available, harvest date, flavour, keeping quality and bearing habit should be recorded as properties of this individual Oak Lodge tree.", provenance: "Species and fruit-tree role are secure; cultivar, pollination group and rootstock remain genuinely unresolved.", family: "Rosaceae", genus: "Malus", foliage: "Deciduous", habit: "Fruit tree; framework depends on training", botanicalLabel: "Identity status", botanicalValue: "Cultivar and rootstock to confirm", role: "Central canopy, bird-feeder support, blossom and fruit", observation: "The tree anchors the replanted bed and carries feeders among its branches.", status: "Photograph mature fruit inside and out, record taste and picking date, and retain any future nursery or graft label.", source: RHS.apples,
